<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.jev-batch — runs `cc-jev batch` on a timer. The batch makes a Jev call ONLY when the
  operator has armed it (`cc-jev arm`); in every other state it writes one line to its log and
  exits 0. Loading this plist therefore schedules nothing that sends anything.

  ⚠️  SHIPS UNLOADED, like com.claude.dispatcher, and for the same house reason: the agent never
  loads launchd. The operator bootstraps it by hand.

      launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.claude.jev-batch.plist
      launchctl bootout   gui/$UID/com.claude.jev-batch        # kill switch

  WHY THE SCHEDULE AND THE CONSENT ARE SEPARATE, which is the whole design:
  a launchd job is NOT bound by the session permission classifier — it is a daemon with no Claude
  Code process in the loop — so a bare timer here would silently convert "the operator ran one
  command" into "our engineering lessons leave this machine on a clock, forever, under standard
  retention". Nobody decided that, and it is a genuine value fork (decision packet, see
  docs/research/jev-100p-2026-09-21/a8-consent-and-scheduling.md). The sentinel keeps the SCHEDULE
  durable and the CONSENT per-window: armed once, consumed before the first call, expiring on a
  clock, carrying the corpus / byte cap / call ceiling it consents to.

  RunAtLoad is TRUE and that is safe precisely because the unarmed path sends nothing — it means
  bootstrapping gives the operator an immediate log line confirming the wiring works, rather than
  a 30-minute wait to find out it does not.

  IT INVOKES `cc-jev batch`, NOT the script directly. 🚨 The reason first written here was FALSE
  and is corrected rather than deleted, because the false version is the more tempting one: it
  said scripts/jev/ "is never deployed to ~/.claude" because install.sh globs scripts/*.sh at the
  top level only. The glob claim is true and the conclusion does not follow — install.sh carries a
  DEDICATED scripts/jev/ block (install.sh:759), written precisely because hooks/lib/jev.sh
  resolves evaluate.mjs relative to its own source dir and jev_available returns false without it.
  Verified after the fact: ~/.claude/scripts/jev/jev-batch.sh is a live symlink. Either spelling
  would therefore work.
  The real reason to prefer cc-jev is smaller and survives: ONE deployed entry point reaches every
  subcommand, so adding a subcommand never needs this plist edited again, and cc-jev resolves its
  own $ROOT through the symlink into the checkout. (That symlink trails origin/main until the live
  layer converges — the com.claude.dispatcher bleed — so a land here still needs deploy-live.)

  StartInterval 1800: the armed window is 180 minutes by default, so a single arming is picked up
  well inside it without the job waking often enough to matter. There is nothing to be timely about
  — the corpus it reads changes on the order of days.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.jev-batch</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"; B="$HOME/.claude/bin/cc-jev"; [ -x "$B" ] || B="$HOME/Development/claude-infrastructure/bin/cc-jev"; exec "$B" batch</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>1800</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>

    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>10</integer>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/autonomy/jev-batch.launchd.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/autonomy/jev-batch.launchd.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
